---
import SimLayout from '../../../layouts/SimLayout.astro';
import Seg from '../../../components/sim/Seg.astro';
import Slider from '../../../components/sim/Slider.astro';
import Checks from '../../../components/sim/Checks.astro';
import Transport from '../../../components/sim/Transport.astro';
import Result from '../../../components/sim/Result.astro';
import Caption from '../../../components/sim/Caption.astro';
import Readout from '../../../components/sim/Readout.astro';
---

<SimLayout
  areaSlug="electronics"
  title="Resistor"
  description="Electrons pulled by the field and knocked back by the lattice, over and over. The average of that crawl is Ohm's law."
  shortcuts={['run', 'reset', 'fullscreen']}
  standard
>
  <div class="sim">
    <div class="sim-view">
      <div class="sim-stage">
        <canvas
          id="res"
          width="1000"
          height="600"
          role="img"
          aria-label="Electrons drifting through a lattice of ions under an electric field, with a plot of their average velocity, the current against voltage, or the heat produced."
        ></canvas>
      </div>
      <Caption
        legend={[
          { label: 'electron', color: '#5eead4' },
          { label: 'lattice ion', color: 'rgba(160,165,175,0.7)' },
          { label: 'collision, heat handed over', color: '#f59e0b' },
        ]}
        hint="The drift is drawn far larger than life; the numbers are the real ones"
      />
      <Result label="Resistance" />
    </div>

    <div class="sim-panel">
      <Transport running />
      <Seg
        id="plot"
        label="Plot under the scene"
        attr="p"
        options={[
          { value: 'scope', label: 'Velocity' },
          { value: 'iv', label: 'I–V' },
          { value: 'power', label: 'Heat' },
        ]}
      />
      <Seg
        id="mat"
        label="Material"
        attr="m"
        active="nichrome"
        options={[
          { value: 'nichrome', label: 'Nichrome' },
          { value: 'copper', label: 'Copper' },
          { value: 'tungsten', label: 'Tungsten' },
          { value: 'carbon', label: 'Carbon' },
        ]}
      />
      <Slider id="v" label="Voltage across it" min={0} max={600} value={100} display="1.00 V" />
      <Slider id="len" label="Length L" min={5} max={200} value={50} display="500 mm" hint={['short, easy', 'long, hard']} />
      <Slider id="area" label="Cross-section A" min={1} max={200} value={10} display="0.10 mm²" hint={['thin, hard', 'fat, easy']} />
      <Slider id="tamb" label="Ambient temperature" min={-50} max={200} value={20} display="20 °C" />
      <Checks
        label="Show"
        items={[
          { id: 'thermal', label: 'Thermal motion', checked: true },
          { id: 'heat', label: 'Let it heat itself up' },
          { id: 'trail', label: 'Follow one electron', checked: true },
        ]}
      />
      <Slider id="rate" label="Time rate" min={10} max={300} step={5} value={100} display="1.0×" />
      <Readout
        title="Values for this metal"
        rows={[
          { id: 'r-R', label: 'Resistance R' },
          { id: 'r-I', label: 'Current I' },
          { id: 'r-P', label: 'Power P' },
          { id: 'r-rho', label: 'Resistivity ρ' },
          { id: 'r-tau', label: 'Mean free time τ' },
          { id: 'r-vd', label: 'Drift speed v<sub>d</sub>' },
          { id: 'r-vth', label: 'Thermal speed' },
          { id: 'r-ratio', label: 'Thermal / drift' },
        ]}
      />
    </div>
  </div>

  <Fragment slot="observe">
    <li>
      Watch a single electron with <b>Follow one electron</b> on. It does not
      sail down the wire. It rattles, gets grabbed by the field for a moment,
      slams into the lattice, forgets which way it was going, and starts over.
      The current is not the speed of that rattling, it is the tiny
      <b>net crawl</b> the rattling has on top of it.
    </li>
    <li>
      Turn <b>thermal motion</b> off. Now the drift is all that is left and it
      is obvious. Turn it back on and it vanishes into the noise, which is
      honest: the thermal speed is around a million times the drift speed. Both
      numbers are in the values list, and the ratio is the reason a wire carrying
      amps still looks like nothing is happening.
    </li>
    <li>
      The graph of average velocity settles onto a flat line. That is the whole
      of Ohm's law: the field <b>accelerates</b>, so without collisions the
      speed would grow forever, but a collision every τ seconds keeps resetting
      it. The average lands at <b>aτ</b>, proportional to the field, and current
      proportional to voltage is nothing but that.
    </li>
    <li>
      Make the sample twice as long. The same voltage is now spread over twice
      the distance, so the field is halved, so the drift is halved, so
      <b>R doubles</b>. Now make it twice as fat instead: same field, same drift,
      but twice as many lanes carrying it, so <b>R halves</b>. That is the whole
      content of R = ρL/A, and neither half of it needed an experiment.
    </li>
    <li>
      Raise the temperature. The lattice shakes harder, an electron gets less
      far before hitting something, τ falls and <b>resistance rises</b>. Carbon
      does the opposite, because heat there frees more carriers than it
      scatters, and that sign difference is why carbon and metal resistors age
      and drift in opposite directions.
    </li>
    <li>
      Every collision hands the field's energy to the lattice, which is where
      <b>P = VI</b> physically goes. Switch on <b>let it heat itself up</b> with
      tungsten and turn the voltage to full. The metal glows, its resistance
      climbs as it does, and on the <b>I–V plot</b> the line <b>bends over</b>:
      a light bulb is not an ohmic device, it just contains one when it is cold.
    </li>
    <li>
      Compare copper with nichrome at the same size. Copper's resistivity is
      about sixty times lower, so at a few volts it draws an absurd current and
      cooks itself. That is why one of them is sold as wire and the other as a
      heating element: <b>same physics, different τ</b>.
    </li>
  </Fragment>
</SimLayout>

<script>
  import { setupCanvas, makeText } from '../../../lib/sim';
  import { stageInk, roundRect, arrow, eng } from '../../../lib/draw';
  import { el, segmented, slider, check, transport, result, readout } from '../../../lib/panel';

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // Drude, taken literally. An electron is accelerated by the field for a while,
  // then a collision randomises its velocity completely. The mean time between
  // collisions is τ, so the average velocity is a·τ = (eE/m)τ, and putting that
  // into I = n e A v_d gives V = IR with R = ρL/A and ρ = m/(n e² τ).
  //
  // The particles on screen run that story in scaled units; the values run it
  // in SI for the metal that is selected. Temperature enters in exactly one
  // place, by shortening τ, which is enough to make a light bulb behave like a
  // light bulb.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

  const E_CHARGE = 1.602e-19;

  interface Material {
    name: string;
    rho20: number; // Ω·m at 20 °C
    alpha: number; // per K
    n: number; // carriers per m³
    melt: number; // °C
  }
  const MATERIALS: Record<string, Material> = {
    copper: { name: 'Copper', rho20: 1.68e-8, alpha: 0.00393, n: 8.5e28, melt: 1085 },
    tungsten: { name: 'Tungsten', rho20: 5.6e-8, alpha: 0.0045, n: 6.3e28, melt: 3422 },
    nichrome: { name: 'Nichrome', rho20: 1.1e-6, alpha: 0.0004, n: 6.0e28, melt: 1400 },
    carbon: { name: 'Carbon', rho20: 3.5e-5, alpha: -0.0005, n: 1.0e25, melt: 3600 },
  };

  const state = {
    mat: 'nichrome',
    plot: 'scope' as 'scope' | 'iv' | 'power',
    V: 1, // volts
    L: 500, // mm
    A: 0.1, // mm²
    tamb: 20, // °C
    thermal: true,
    heat: false,
    trail: true,
    rate: 1,
    running: true,
  };
  let T = 20; // the sample's own temperature, °C

  const mat = () => MATERIALS[state.mat];
  const rho = () => mat().rho20 * (1 + mat().alpha * (T - 20));
  const Rof = () => Math.max(1e-9, (rho() * (state.L * 1e-3)) / (state.A * 1e-6));
  const current = () => state.V / Rof();
  const power = () => state.V * current();
  const field = () => state.V / (state.L * 1e-3); // V/m
  /** Drift speed from the current, which is the same thing as aτ. */
  const vdrift = () => current() / (mat().n * E_CHARGE * state.A * 1e-6);
  /** Thermal speed, room temperature order of magnitude. */
  const vthermal = () => 1.1e5 * Math.sqrt((T + 273) / 293);
  /** τ read back out of the resistivity, since ρ = m/(n e² τ). */
  const tau = () => 9.11e-31 / (mat().n * E_CHARGE * E_CHARGE * rho());

  // --- thermal balance -------------------------------------------------------

  const SIGMA = 5.67e-8;
  const surface = () => 4 * Math.sqrt(state.A * 1e-6) * state.L * 1e-3; // m²
  const heatLost = () =>
    25 * surface() * (T - state.tamb) + 0.4 * SIGMA * surface() * ((T + 273) ** 4 - (state.tamb + 273) ** 4);
  function thermalStep(dt: number) {
    if (!state.heat) {
      T += (state.tamb - T) * Math.min(1, dt * 6);
      return;
    }
    const vol = state.L * 1e-3 * state.A * 1e-6; // m³
    const c = 3.5e6; // J/(m³·K), near enough for a metal
    const dT = ((power() - heatLost()) / (c * vol)) * dt;
    T = Math.max(-273, T + Math.max(-4000 * dt, Math.min(4000 * dt, dT)));
  }

  // --- the particle picture --------------------------------------------------

  const SLAB = { x: 20, y: 16, w: 960, h: 300 };
  let box = { x: 0, y: 0, w: 0, h: 0 };

  type Electron = { x: number; y: number; vx: number; vy: number };
  let elec: Electron[] = [];
  let trail: { x: number; y: number }[] = [];
  let flashes: { x: number; y: number; life: number }[] = [];
  let vbar = 0; // measured mean vx, px/s
  let vhist: number[] = [];

  const rnd = (() => {
    let s = 424242;
    return () => ((s = (s * 1664525 + 1013904223) >>> 0) / 4294967296);
  })();

  function geometry() {
    // Drawn to scale: longer samples stretch, fatter ones thicken, so R = ρL/A
    // is a shape you can see and not only a formula.
    const w = 240 + (state.L / 2000) * 560;
    const h = 56 + Math.sqrt(state.A / 2) * 130;
    box = { x: SLAB.x + (SLAB.w - w) / 2, y: SLAB.y + (SLAB.h - h) / 2 + 6, w, h };
  }

  function seed() {
    geometry();
    elec = [];
    for (let k = 0; k < 190; k++)
      elec.push({ x: box.x + rnd() * box.w, y: box.y + rnd() * box.h, vx: 0, vy: 0 });
    trail = [];
    flashes = [];
    vhist = [];
    vbar = 0;
    T = state.tamb;
  }

  const VTH = 150; // px/s, the scaled thermal speed
  const TAU0 = 0.075; // s, the scaled mean free time at 20 °C
  const scaledAccel = () => Math.min(2600, (field() / 40) * 1.4);
  const scaledTau = () => TAU0 / Math.max(0.15, 1 + mat().alpha * (T - 20));

  function scatter(e: Electron) {
    const a = rnd() * Math.PI * 2;
    const s = state.thermal ? VTH * (0.5 + rnd()) * Math.sqrt((T + 273) / 293) : 0;
    e.vx = Math.cos(a) * s;
    e.vy = Math.sin(a) * s;
  }

  function particles(dt: number) {
    const accel = scaledAccel(), tauS = scaledTau();
    let sum = 0;
    for (let i = 0; i < elec.length; i++) {
      const e = elec[i];
      e.vx += accel * dt;
      e.x += e.vx * dt;
      e.y += e.vy * dt;
      if (e.y < box.y) { e.y = box.y; e.vy = Math.abs(e.vy); }
      if (e.y > box.y + box.h) { e.y = box.y + box.h; e.vy = -Math.abs(e.vy); }
      if (e.x > box.x + box.w) { e.x = box.x; if (i === 0) trail = []; }
      if (e.x < box.x) { e.x = box.x + box.w; if (i === 0) trail = []; }
      if (rnd() < dt / tauS) {
        if (flashes.length < 40) flashes.push({ x: e.x, y: e.y, life: 1 });
        scatter(e);
      }
      sum += e.vx;
    }
    vbar += (sum / elec.length - vbar) * Math.min(1, dt * 3);
    vhist.push(vbar);
    if (vhist.length > 460) vhist.shift();
    if (state.trail) {
      trail.push({ x: elec[0].x, y: elec[0].y });
      if (trail.length > 320) trail.shift();
    }
    for (const f of flashes) f.life -= dt * 2.4;
    flashes = flashes.filter((f) => f.life > 0);
  }

  // --- canvas ----------------------------------------------------------------

  const canvas = el('res') as HTMLCanvasElement;
  const { ctx, W, H } = setupCanvas(canvas, () => draw());
  const { ink } = stageInk(canvas);
  const { label } = makeText(ctx, ink);
  const setResult = result();
  const setRows = readout();

  const ACC = '#7c8cff'; // the field
  const TEAL = '#5eead4'; // electrons
  const WARM = '#f59e0b'; // heat
  const GREEN = '#34d399'; // current
  const HOT = '#f9736a';

  // One plot at a time under the scene; the panel picks which.
  const PLOT = { x: 20, y: 350, w: 960, h: 230 };

  /** How hot metal looks, roughly: dull red, orange, then white. */
  function glow(t: number) {
    const f = Math.max(0, Math.min(1, (t - 500) / 1600));
    if (f <= 0) return null;
    return { css: `rgb(255,${Math.round(60 + 180 * f)},${Math.round(20 + 190 * f * f)})`, a: 0.12 + 0.55 * f };
  }
  const fmtR = (r: number) =>
    r >= 1e6 ? (r / 1e6).toFixed(2) + ' MΩ'
    : r >= 1e3 ? (r / 1e3).toFixed(2) + ' kΩ'
    : r >= 1 ? r.toFixed(2) + ' Ω'
    : r >= 1e-3 ? (r * 1e3).toFixed(2) + ' mΩ'
    : (r * 1e6).toFixed(1) + ' µΩ';

  // --- the slab --------------------------------------------------------------

  function drawSlab() {
    geometry();

    // terminals
    ctx.fillStyle = ink(0.28);
    roundRect(ctx, box.x - 44, box.y - 12, 44, box.h + 24, 4);
    ctx.fill();
    roundRect(ctx, box.x + box.w, box.y - 12, 44, box.h + 24, 4);
    ctx.fill();
    label('+', box.x - 22, box.y + box.h / 2 + 6, 1, 'center', 17, WARM);
    label('−', box.x + box.w + 22, box.y + box.h / 2 + 6, 1, 'center', 17, ACC);

    // the sample itself, glowing if it is hot enough to
    const g = glow(T);
    ctx.fillStyle = g ? g.css : ink(0.04);
    ctx.globalAlpha = g ? g.a : 1;
    ctx.fillRect(box.x, box.y, box.w, box.h);
    ctx.globalAlpha = 1;
    ctx.strokeStyle = ink(0.25);
    ctx.lineWidth = 1;
    ctx.strokeRect(box.x, box.y, box.w, box.h);

    // the lattice, shaking harder the hotter it is
    const jitter = Math.min(2.4, 0.25 + Math.sqrt(Math.max(0, T + 273) / 293) * 0.55);
    const step = 44, t = performance.now() / 220;
    ctx.fillStyle = ink(0.34);
    for (let x = box.x + step / 2; x < box.x + box.w; x += step)
      for (let y = box.y + step / 2; y < box.y + box.h; y += step) {
        ctx.beginPath();
        ctx.arc(x + Math.sin(t + x * 0.7 + y * 0.3) * jitter, y + Math.cos(t * 1.13 + x * 0.4 - y * 0.6) * jitter, 3.4, 0, Math.PI * 2);
        ctx.fill();
      }

    // the field that does the pushing
    ctx.strokeStyle = ACC;
    ctx.fillStyle = ACC;
    ctx.lineWidth = 1.6;
    const fy = box.y + box.h + 30;
    for (let k = 0; k < 5; k++) {
      const x0 = box.x + 24 + (k * (box.w - 48)) / 4;
      arrow(ctx, x0 - 16, fy, x0 + 16, fy, 5);
    }
    label(`E = V / L = ${eng(field(), 'V/m', 1)}`, box.x + box.w / 2, fy + 20, 1, 'center', 11.5, ACC);

    // the collisions, where the energy is handed over
    for (const f of flashes) {
      ctx.strokeStyle = `rgba(245,158,11,${(f.life * 0.5).toFixed(3)})`;
      ctx.lineWidth = 1.2;
      ctx.beginPath();
      ctx.arc(f.x, f.y, 4 + (1 - f.life) * 9, 0, Math.PI * 2);
      ctx.stroke();
    }

    // one electron's path, so the zigzag is not just an assertion
    if (state.trail && trail.length > 2) {
      ctx.strokeStyle = 'rgba(94,234,212,0.55)';
      ctx.lineWidth = 1.4;
      ctx.beginPath();
      trail.forEach((p, k) => (k ? ctx.lineTo(p.x, p.y) : ctx.moveTo(p.x, p.y)));
      ctx.stroke();
    }
    for (let i = 0; i < elec.length; i++) {
      const e = elec[i];
      const followed = i === 0 && state.trail;
      ctx.fillStyle = followed ? '#ffffff' : TEAL;
      ctx.beginPath();
      ctx.arc(e.x, e.y, followed ? 4.4 : 3, 0, Math.PI * 2);
      ctx.fill();
    }

    // the geometry, named
    ctx.strokeStyle = ink(0.3);
    ctx.fillStyle = ink(0.3);
    ctx.lineWidth = 1;
    const ly = box.y - 22;
    arrow(ctx, box.x, ly, box.x + box.w, ly, 5);
    arrow(ctx, box.x + box.w, ly, box.x, ly, 5);
    label(state.L >= 1000 ? `L = ${(state.L / 1000).toFixed(2)} m` : `L = ${state.L} mm`, box.x + box.w / 2, ly - 8, 0.55, 'center', 11);
    label(`A = ${state.A.toFixed(2)} mm²`, box.x - 52, box.y + box.h / 2 + 4, 0.55, 'right', 11);
  }

  // --- the three plots -------------------------------------------------------

  // The bent curve of a self-heating sample is only visible if we remember
  // where it has been, so keep the points it has actually sat at.
  let ivTrace: { v: number; i: number }[] = [];

  function drawIV() {
    label('current against voltage', PLOT.x + 4, PLOT.y - 2, 0.45, 'left', 10.5);
    const px = PLOT.x + 46, py = PLOT.y + 18, pw = PLOT.w - 62, ph = PLOT.h - 56;
    const vmax = 6;
    const imax = Math.max(current(), ...ivTrace.map((p) => p.i), 1e-9) * 1.15;
    const X = (v: number) => px + (v / vmax) * pw;
    const Y = (i: number) => py + ph - (i / imax) * ph;

    ctx.strokeStyle = ink(0.18);
    ctx.lineWidth = 1;
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.moveTo(px, py);
    ctx.lineTo(px, py + ph);
    ctx.lineTo(px + pw, py + ph);
    ctx.stroke();
    label('V', px + pw, py + ph + 16, 0.35, 'right', 10);
    label('I', px - 8, py + 8, 0.35, 'right', 10);
    label(eng(imax, 'A', 1), px - 8, py + 20, 0.3, 'right', 9);

    // the straight line the sample would follow if R never moved
    const R0 = (mat().rho20 * (1 + mat().alpha * (state.tamb - 20)) * state.L * 1e-3) / (state.A * 1e-6);
    ctx.strokeStyle = ink(0.28);
    ctx.setLineDash([4, 4]);
    ctx.lineWidth = 1.4;
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.moveTo(X(0), Y(0));
    ctx.lineTo(X(vmax), Y(vmax / R0));
    ctx.stroke();
    ctx.setLineDash([]);
    label('ohmic, cold', px + pw - 4, py + 14, 0.4, 'right', 10);

    if (ivTrace.length > 1) {
      ctx.strokeStyle = GREEN;
      ctx.lineWidth = 2;
      ctx.beginPath();
      ivTrace.forEach((p, k) => (k ? ctx.lineTo(X(p.v), Y(p.i)) : ctx.moveTo(X(p.v), Y(p.i))));
      ctx.stroke();
    }
    ctx.fillStyle = GREEN;
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.arc(X(state.V), Y(current()), 5, 0, Math.PI * 2);
    ctx.fill();
  }

  function drawScope() {
    label('average velocity of all the electrons', PLOT.x + 4, PLOT.y - 2, 0.45, 'left', 10.5);
    const px = PLOT.x + 42, py = PLOT.y + 20, pw = PLOT.w - 56, ph = PLOT.h - 50;
    const pred = scaledAccel() * scaledTau();
    const scale = Math.max(pred * 1.9, ...vhist.map((v) => Math.abs(v) * 1.25), 12);
    const Y = (v: number) => py + ph - (v / scale) * ph;

    ctx.strokeStyle = ink(0.18);
    ctx.lineWidth = 1;
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.moveTo(px, py);
    ctx.lineTo(px, py + ph);
    ctx.lineTo(px + pw, py + ph);
    ctx.stroke();

    ctx.strokeStyle = ACC;
    ctx.setLineDash([5, 4]);
    ctx.lineWidth = 1.5;
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.moveTo(px, Y(pred));
    ctx.lineTo(px + pw, Y(pred));
    ctx.stroke();
    ctx.setLineDash([]);
    label('a τ, predicted', px + pw + 2, Y(pred) - 6, 1, 'right', 11, ACC);

    if (vhist.length > 1) {
      ctx.strokeStyle = TEAL;
      ctx.lineWidth = 2;
      ctx.beginPath();
      vhist.forEach((v, k) => (k ? ctx.lineTo(px + (k / (vhist.length - 1)) * pw, Y(v)) : ctx.moveTo(px, Y(v))));
      ctx.stroke();
    }
    label('measured', px + 6, py + 12, 1, 'left', 10.5, TEAL);
  }

  function drawPower() {
    label('where the energy goes', PLOT.x + 4, PLOT.y - 2, 0.45, 'left', 10.5);
    const px = PLOT.x + 22, py = PLOT.y + 24;
    label('P = V I = I²R', px, py + 6, 1, 'left', 14, WARM);
    label(eng(power(), 'W'), px + PLOT.w - 44, py + 6, 1, 'right', 15, WARM);

    // a temperature column, with the melting point drawn as a wall
    const bx = px, by = py + 32, bw = PLOT.w - 60, bh = 20;
    const tmax = Math.max(mat().melt * 1.15, 400);
    ctx.fillStyle = ink(0.07);
    roundRect(ctx, bx, by, bw, bh, 5);
    ctx.fill();
    const f = Math.max(0, Math.min(1, (T + 50) / (tmax + 50)));
    const g = glow(T);
    ctx.fillStyle = g ? g.css : WARM;
    roundRect(ctx, bx, by, Math.max(3, f * bw), bh, 5);
    ctx.fill();
    const mx = bx + ((mat().melt + 50) / (tmax + 50)) * bw;
    ctx.strokeStyle = HOT;
    ctx.lineWidth = 2;
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.moveTo(mx, by - 5);
    ctx.lineTo(mx, by + bh + 5);
    ctx.stroke();
    label('melts', mx + 5, by + bh + 18, 1, 'left', 10, HOT);
    label(`${T.toFixed(0)} °C`, bx, by + bh + 18, 1, 'left', 12, WARM);

    // the balance that decides where it settles
    const yy = by + 56;
    label(state.heat ? 'in, from the field' : 'held at ambient', px, yy, 0.5, 'left', 11);
    label(state.heat ? eng(power(), 'W') : '—', px + PLOT.w - 44, yy, 1, 'right', 11, GREEN);
    label('out, to the air and as light', px, yy + 20, 0.5, 'left', 11);
    label(state.heat ? eng(heatLost(), 'W') : '—', px + PLOT.w - 44, yy + 20, 1, 'right', 11, ACC);
    label(`resistivity now ${eng(rho(), 'Ω·m', 2)}`, px, yy + 46, 0.45, 'left', 10.5);
  }

  // --- the words and numbers -------------------------------------------------

  function words() {
    const melted = T > mat().melt;
    const note =
      melted ? 'Past the melting point: this sample no longer exists.'
      : state.plot === 'iv' ? (state.heat ? 'The I–V line bends because R follows the heat.' : 'A straight I–V line, and that is Ohm.')
      : state.plot === 'power' ? (state.heat ? 'The temperature settles where heat in and heat out balance.' : 'Switch on self-heating to watch the temperature climb.')
      : 'The average velocity settles at aτ, proportional to the field. That flat line is Ohm’s law.';
    setResult({
      value: fmtR(Rof()),
      colour: melted ? HOT : undefined,
      status: `${mat().name} · I = ${eng(current(), 'A')} · P = ${eng(power(), 'W')} · ${T.toFixed(0)} °C`,
      note,
    });
    setRows({
      'r-R': fmtR(Rof()),
      'r-I': eng(current(), 'A'),
      'r-P': eng(power(), 'W'),
      'r-rho': eng(rho(), 'Ω·m'),
      'r-tau': eng(tau(), 's'),
      'r-vd': eng(vdrift(), 'm/s'),
      'r-vth': eng(vthermal(), 'm/s', 1),
      'r-ratio': vdrift() > 0 ? (vthermal() / vdrift()).toExponential(1) + '×' : '—',
    });
  }

  // --- frame -----------------------------------------------------------------

  function draw() {
    ctx.clearRect(0, 0, W, H);
    drawSlab();
    if (state.plot === 'iv') drawIV();
    else if (state.plot === 'power') drawPower();
    else drawScope();
  }

  let last = performance.now();
  function frame(now: number) {
    const dt = Math.min(0.05, Math.max(0, (now - last) / 1000)) * state.rate;
    last = now;
    if (state.running) {
      particles(dt);
      thermalStep(dt);
      const i = current();
      const prev = ivTrace[ivTrace.length - 1];
      if (!prev || Math.abs(prev.v - state.V) > 1e-3 || Math.abs(prev.i - i) / (i || 1) > 5e-3) {
        ivTrace.push({ v: state.V, i });
        if (ivTrace.length > 400) ivTrace.shift();
      }
    }
    draw();
    words();
    requestAnimationFrame(frame);
  }

  // --- controls --------------------------------------------------------------

  transport({
    onRun: (running) => (state.running = running),
    onReset: () => { ivTrace = []; seed(); },
  });
  segmented('plot', (p) => (state.plot = p as typeof state.plot));
  segmented('mat', (m) => { state.mat = m; ivTrace = []; seed(); });

  slider('v', (n) => (state.V = n / 100), (n) => `${(n / 100).toFixed(2)} V`);
  slider('len', (n) => { state.L = n * 10; geometry(); }, (n) => (n * 10 >= 1000 ? `${(n / 100).toFixed(2)} m` : `${n * 10} mm`));
  slider('area', (n) => { state.A = n / 100; geometry(); }, (n) => `${(n / 100).toFixed(2)} mm²`);
  slider('tamb', (n) => (state.tamb = n), (n) => `${n} °C`);
  slider('rate', (n) => (state.rate = n / 100), (n) => `${(n / 100).toFixed(1)}×`);
  check('thermal', (on) => (state.thermal = on));
  check('heat', (on) => { state.heat = on; ivTrace = []; });
  check('trail', (on) => { state.trail = on; trail = []; });

  seed();
  requestAnimationFrame(frame);
</script>

<style>
  #res {
    touch-action: none;
  }
</style>
